# notifier-pipe — Cursor Rules (Challenge Constraints)

## Project Nature
- This is a Java LIBRARY, not an application.
- Keep the core framework-agnostic and usable from any Java project.

## Hard Prohibitions (DO NOT)
- Do NOT use frameworks that force architecture (e.g., Spring, Quarkus, Micronaut).
- Do NOT use DI containers or framework-specific annotations (@Component, @Service, etc.).
- Do NOT use external configuration files (YAML, .properties, application.conf, etc.).
- Do NOT implement real HTTP/provider integrations; simulate sending instead.

## Required (MUST)
- Java version: 21 or higher.
- Build tool: Maven.
- Mandatory channels supported: Email, Push Notification, SMS.
- Apply SOLID principles (focus on OCP, DIP, SRP).
- Provide unit tests using mocks/stubs/simulations (no real network calls).
- Provide a README.md for another developer:
  - Installation (Maven/Gradle)
  - Quick Start
  - Configuration via Java code
  - Supported providers (conceptually)
  - API reference
  - Security best practices for credentials

## Allowed (OK to use)
- Utility libraries that do NOT force architecture:
  - Lombok (e.g., @Getter, @Builder)
  - Jackson/Gson
  - SLF4J
  - Apache Commons
- Logging/console output for simulated sending.

## Design Expectations (DO)
- Provide a unified interface/entry point for sending notifications across channels.
- Make provider switching transparent to library consumers.
- Make it easy to add new channels/providers without modifying existing core code.

## Optional (Nice-to-have)
- Dockerfile for demo execution
- Async sending using CompletableFuture (including batch)
- Validation (email/phone)
- Templates
- Notification status events (pub/sub or similar)

## Output Quality
- Prefer clean, minimal public API (stable and hard to break).
- Keep examples small and runnable; avoid framework setup.
- Do not introduce complexity unless required by the challenge.
